; Tutorial 4.02: Waves
;
; Water here is a flat grid of triangles whose corners move every frame. A few waves that run in different
; directions, added together, give the swell. A ring that spreads out from a point gives the ripple of a stone
; dropped into the water. After moving the corners, UpdateNormals works out which way each part of the surface now
; faces, so the light shades the waves.
;
; The simplest wave only moves each corner up and down along a sine curve, which gives round, soft hills. Real water
; looks different: its crests are sharp and its troughs wide and flat. Gerstner waves get that by also moving each
; corner forward and back. Every bit of water goes round in a small circle: forward at the top, back at the bottom.
; So the corners crowd together at the crests and spread out in the troughs. G switches between the two kinds, so
; the difference can be seen.
;
; For one wave, with "angle" the place of the corner along the wave (in degrees, since Sin and Cos use them):
;
;   height    = amplitude * Sin(angle)
;   sideways  = steepness * amplitude * Cos(angle), along the direction that the wave runs in
;
; The higher the steepness, the sharper the crests. Too high, and the water folds over itself at the crests: the sum of
; steepness times amplitude times 2 * Pi / length over all waves must stay below 1.
;
; New things here:
;   A grid mesh made with AddVertex and AddTriangle (see tutorial 3.03), changed every frame with VertexCoords.
;   UpdateNormals, so the lighting follows the moving surface.
;   Gerstner waves, which move the water sideways as well as up and down.
;   One function used twice: for the water and for the buoys, so the buoys ride on the waves.
;   EntityShininess for a glossy highlight.
;   Picking an invisible flat helper mesh to find where the mouse points on the water.
;
; Controls: click the water to drop a stone, Up / Down change the wind (how high the swell is), G switches between
; Gerstner and plain sine waves, Left / Right turn the camera.

Include "<keys.bb>"
Include "<mouse.bb>"
Include "<graphics.bb>"
Include "<entity.bb>"

Graphics3D 640, 480, 0, BB_GRAPHICS_WINDOWED
AppTitle "Tutorial 4.02: Waves"
SetBuffer BackBuffer()
SeedRnd MilliSecs()

Const GRID = 64       ; Corners along each side of the water.
Const SIZE# = 40.0    ; Width of the water in units.

Local cameraPivot = CreatePivot()
Global camera = CreateCamera(cameraPivot)
PositionEntity camera, 0, 5, -14.0
RotateEntity camera, 18, 0, 0
CameraClsColor camera, 150, 190, 220
CameraFogMode camera, 1
CameraFogRange camera, 10, 24
CameraFogColor camera, 150, 190, 220

Local sun = CreateLight()
RotateEntity sun, 20, -60, 0
AmbientLight 35, 45, 60

; --- The water mesh --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; GRID x GRID corners, two triangles for every square between them. The corners' x and z never change, so they are
; kept in arrays; only the height is worked out again every frame.
Global water = CreateMesh()
Global waterSurface = CreateSurface(water)
Dim cornerX#(GRID * GRID - 1), cornerZ#(GRID * GRID - 1)
Local x, z, i
For z = 0 To GRID - 1
	For x = 0 To GRID - 1
		i = z * GRID + x
		cornerX(i) = (Float(x) / (GRID - 1) - 0.5) * SIZE
		cornerZ(i) = (Float(z) / (GRID - 1) - 0.5) * SIZE
		AddVertex waterSurface, cornerX(i), 0, cornerZ(i)
	Next
Next
For z = 0 To GRID - 2
	For x = 0 To GRID - 2
		i = z * GRID + x
		AddTriangle waterSurface, i, i + GRID, i + 1
		AddTriangle waterSurface, i + 1, i + GRID, i + GRID + 1
	Next
Next
EntityColor water, 30, 95, 140
EntityShininess water, 0.9

; A flat, invisible copy of the water for picking. Picking the moving mesh would also work, but the flat one is
; simpler: the stone lands where the mouse points on the calm surface.
Local pickPlane = CreateCube()
ScaleEntity pickPlane, SIZE / 2, 0.01, SIZE / 2
EntityAlpha pickPlane, 0
EntityPickMode pickPlane, BB_PICK_POLYGON

; --- Buoys -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Type Buoy
	Field entity
	Field x#, z#
End Type

Local b.Buoy, top
For i = 1 To 5
	b = New Buoy
	b\x = Rnd(-8, 8)
	b\z = Rnd(-6, 8)
	b\entity = CreateCylinder(12)
	ScaleEntity b\entity, 0.3, 0.5, 0.3
	EntityColor b\entity, 230, 60, 40
	top = CreateSphere(8, b\entity)
	PositionEntity top, 0, 1.2, 0
	ScaleEntity top, 0.7, 0.4, 0.7
	EntityColor top, 240, 240, 240
Next

; --- Ripples ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Type Ripple
	Field x#, z#
	Field age#   ; Seconds since the stone landed.
End Type

Global time#, wind# = 0.5, gerstner = True
Const STEEPNESS# = 1.6 ; With the waves below at full wind: 1.6 * 0.55 = 0.89, sharp but not folded over.

; --- The waves -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Three waves. Each has a direction, a length (in units from crest to crest), a height and a speed (in degrees of
; angle per second). Long waves are high and slow, short ones low and fast, like on the sea.
Const WAVES = 3
Dim waveDirX#(WAVES - 1), waveDirZ#(WAVES - 1), waveNumber#(WAVES - 1), waveAmplitude#(WAVES - 1), waveSpeed#(WAVES - 1)
Restore WaveData
Local length#, dirX#, dirZ#, dirLength#
For i = 0 To WAVES - 1
	Read dirX, dirZ, length, waveAmplitude(i), waveSpeed(i)
	dirLength = Sqr(dirX * dirX + dirZ * dirZ)
	waveDirX(i) = dirX / dirLength
	waveDirZ(i) = dirZ / dirLength
	waveNumber(i) = 360 / length ; Degrees of angle per unit, so one whole Sin every "length" units.
Next

; WaterPoint puts the place of the water that rests at (x, z) into these three.
Global waterX#, waterY#, waterZ#

Local r.Ripple, h#, ahead#, slope#, ripples, buoyX#, buoyZ#
While Not KeyHit(BB_KEY_ESCAPE)
	time = time + 1.0 / 60
	TurnEntity cameraPivot, 0, (KeyDown(BB_KEY_LEFT) - KeyDown(BB_KEY_RIGHT)) * 1.0, 0
	If KeyDown(BB_KEY_UP) Then wind = wind + 0.005
	If KeyDown(BB_KEY_DOWN) Then wind = wind - 0.005
	If wind < 0 Then wind = 0
	If wind > 1 Then wind = 1
	If KeyHit(BB_KEY_G) Then gerstner = Not gerstner

	; A click on the water drops a stone where the mouse points.
	If MouseHit(BB_MOUSE_LEFT)
		If CameraPick(camera, Float(MouseX()), Float(MouseY())) = pickPlane
			r = New Ripple
			r\x = PickedX()
			r\z = PickedZ()
		EndIf
	EndIf
	ripples = 0
	For r = Each Ripple
		r\age = r\age + 1.0 / 60
		If r\age > 6 Then Delete r Else ripples = ripples + 1
	Next

	; Move every corner to where its bit of water is now, then let UpdateNormals fix the lighting.
	For i = 0 To GRID * GRID - 1
		WaterPoint(cornerX(i), cornerZ(i))
		VertexCoords waterSurface, i, waterX, waterY, waterZ
	Next
	UpdateNormals water

	; The buoys move with the bit of water they float on, so with Gerstner waves they also go round in small circles.
	; They lean with the slope: compare the height a little to the front and to the side of the buoy with the height
	; under it.
	For b = Each Buoy
		WaterPoint(b\x, b\z)
		h = waterY : buoyX = waterX : buoyZ = waterZ
		WaterPoint(b\x, b\z + 0.5)
		ahead = waterY
		WaterPoint(b\x + 0.5, b\z)
		slope = waterY
		PositionEntity b\entity, buoyX, h, buoyZ
		RotateEntity b\entity, ATan2(ahead - h, 0.5) * -1, 0, ATan2(slope - h, 0.5)
	Next

	RenderWorld
	Color 20, 40, 60
	Text 10, 10, "Click the water to drop a stone. Up / Down: wind " + Int(wind * 100) + "%."
	If gerstner Then Text 10, 30, "G: Gerstner waves, sharp crests." Else Text 10, 30, "G: plain sine waves, round hills."
	Text 10, 50, "Left / Right turn the camera. Ripples: " + ripples
	Flip
Wend
End

; Where the water that rests at (x, z) is now, in waterX, waterY and waterZ. Everything moving on the water asks
; this function, so it all agrees.
Function WaterPoint(x#, z#)
	waterX = x : waterY = 0 : waterZ = z

	; The swell. Each wave adds its height, and for Gerstner waves also its push along its direction.
	Local i, angle#, amplitude#
	For i = 0 To WAVES - 1
		angle = (x * waveDirX(i) + z * waveDirZ(i)) * waveNumber(i) - time * waveSpeed(i)
		amplitude = waveAmplitude(i) * wind
		waterY = waterY + amplitude * Sin(angle)
		If gerstner
			waterX = waterX + STEEPNESS * amplitude * waveDirX(i) * Cos(angle)
			waterZ = waterZ + STEEPNESS * amplitude * waveDirZ(i) * Cos(angle)
		EndIf
	Next

	Local h# = 0
	; Ripples: a ring that moves out at 4 units a second. Only the water the ring has already reached moves, and the
	; ripple gets weaker with time and with distance.
	Local r.Ripple, dx#, dz#, distance#, front#
	For r = Each Ripple
		dx = x - r\x
		dz = z - r\z
		distance = Sqr(dx * dx + dz * dz)
		front = r\age * 4
		If distance < front
			h = h + Sin((distance - front) * 120) * 0.9 * (1 - r\age / 6) / (1 + distance * 0.3)
		EndIf
	Next
	waterY = waterY + h
End Function

; Direction x and z, length from crest to crest, height, speed in degrees per second. The sum of height * 2 * Pi /
; length over all waves is about 0.55 at full wind, which leaves room for the steepness.
.WaveData
Data 1.0, 0.0, 14.4, 0.5, 60.0
Data 0.3, 1.0, 9.9, 0.3, 90.0
Data 1.0, -1.0, 5.1, 0.12, 140.0
